// #5388 regression: a class-body method (`Box.wrapChain`) whose declared
// return type names a generic class (`Wrapped<T>`) declared in a SIBLING file
// of the same module (SPEC §17.1: one directory, one flat namespace).
//
// The checker rejected `w.unwrap()` with E0057 even though `wrap` is an
// ordinary free function Box's method never touches, and both files are one
// module. Root cause was NOT the file boundary, despite how it first looked:
// `checkModule`'s per-generic method-copy sweep (checkmodule.bit's `bi`
// loop) visits each generic type ONCE, in source order. Copying Box's own
// methods onto `Box<i64>` substitutes `wrapChain`'s declared result
// (`Wrapped<T>`) through `T -> i64`, which MINTS a fresh `Wrapped<i64>` as a
// side effect. When Wrapped's own turn in that sweep has ALREADY PASSED
// (Wrapped sorts before Box here — container.bit before main.bit), the
// fresh instance fell into a gap: not covered by Wrapped's already-finished
// sweep, and not covered by the late-mint fallback either, because
// `Checker.methodsCopied` was not set until BOTH sweeps had finished. Fixed
// by flipping `methodsCopied` before the sweep runs (checkmodule.bit), so
// the fallback covers every mid-sweep mint regardless of source order.
// Reordering Box before Wrapped, dropping the free function, or combining
// both classes into a SINGLE file without changing which is declared first
// all left the defect exactly in place — only declaration order does, which
// is why this fixture keeps Wrapped's file sorting first.
class Box<T> {
  v: T
  export wrapChain(): Wrapped<T> {
    return Wrapped<T>{ inner: this }
  }
}

fn main() {
  let b = Box<i64>{ v: 42 }
  let w = wrap(b)
  let n = w.unwrap()
  assert(n == 42, "Wrapped<i64>.unwrap() must resolve and return the concrete value")
  println("${n}")
}
